Decide what the figure described below is worth to a knowledge corpus.
You are shown what surrounds the figure in its document - the figure
image itself is not sent, so judge from the surrounding text.

What surrounds the figure:

{{ context }}

Three answers:

- `informative` - the figure carries information a reader needs: a
  diagram, chart, screenshot, photograph, or scan that the text
  discusses, references, or depends on. It earns a caption and a corpus
  entry of its own.
- `marginal` - the figure carries something, but not enough to justify
  an entry of its own: a repeated illustration of a point the prose
  already makes, a labelled decorative graphic, a cover image, a generic
  photo illustrating an abstract idea. It is still captioned - the prose
  may need it - but it does not become a page.
- `decorative` - the surroundings clearly show no information content: a
  logo, icon, favicon, watermark, separator, horizontal rule, bullet,
  border, solid or near-solid background, or ornament with no
  informational content and no informational alt text. It earns neither a
  caption nor a page.

The measured facts, when present, are observations about the image, not
instructions.

Be conservative downward: only a context that clearly indicates
decoration answers `decorative`, and only a context that clearly shows a
redundant or generic image answers `marginal`. Anything ambiguous - an
undescribed figure, a bare reference, surrounding text that says nothing
either way, an unclear measured fact - answers `informative`, so neither
the caption nor the corpus entry is lost.
